<handout>  
    <title>Challenge Problem 4: Tuned Mass Dampers</title>
    <objectives>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                         Categorizing your struggles with a problem (applied problem solving)

                        </p>
                    </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Discovering for getting unstuck when you're stuck (applied problem solving)
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Turning a vague confusion into a specific question (communication dexterity)
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Resolving conflicting arguments: I know there's an error, but where? (critical skills)
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
   </objectives>
  <page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>General Advice and Logistics</title>
        
            <p>
                <alert>Logistics:</alert> Challenge problems are collaborative. You are required to work in groups.
                Individual submissions will not be accepted, so please make sure to find a partner ahead of time.
                Please contact the instructor if you are having trouble finding a partner. 
            </p>

            <p>
                Starting on your own is encouraged, but articulating your arguments, questions, and understanding 
                those of your classmates is one of the key skills this course seeks to develop. If you cannot clearly
                explain your argument, or are thrown off by questions, your understanding is not solid. 
            </p>

            <p>
                <alert>Some tips:</alert> The hardest thing to fix is a blank page. Start with drawing a careful 
                picture; writing everything that's given; everything that's unknown; the thing you're looking for; 
                then writing all the things you know are true, labeled by why they are true.
            </p>
            <p>
            </p>

            <p>
                <alert>Perfectionism reminder:</alert> the goal of the challenge problems is to challenge you. 
                This means that they will be hard, and when you turn them in for the first time you may not be sure
                if your solution is correct. That's OK: you will get feedback on your solution and you will have a 
                chance to resubmit, taking the feedback into account. Furthermore, we are not looking for perfection 
                (but you should strive for it anyway!). I don't mean to say you should just throw something at the
                wall and see if it sticks. Just remember that the challenge is part of the process and it'll lead 
                to growth.
            </p>

            <p>
                <alert>Sanity checks:</alert> Once you have solved the problem, check that your solution makes sense.
                We will ask you to do this over and over, because it is an important skill to develop. When you
                submit your design for a bridge, you better be sure it won't collapse. When you publish a paper with
                your major theoretical breakthrough or experimental discovery, you want to be sure that you are right.
                Perhaps a way of thinking about this is as follows: try to find errors in your solution. Are the
                units right? If I make a particular variable go to zero or infinity, does the answer behave
                in the expected way? If I plug in reasonable numerical values, do I get a reasonable result?
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Academic Integrity:</alert> Looking up solutions to this problem to see if it's already been solved, 
                as well as using AI tools to generate solutions/ideas for the problems, is not allowed. Doing this 
                is not only a serious ethica violation (presenting work that is not your own as if it were), but it
                short-circuits the valuable learning  process of thinking hard about something and refining your 
                thinking until you get it right. If you need help, talk to one of your instructors, or 
                collaborate with another group. You have two chances! Believe in yourself and take it slowly.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Context for the Problem</title>
            <p>
                The resonance phenomenon can be harnessed for many useful applications <mdash/> 
                but can also be destructive, destroying bridges or toppling buildings. 
                For example, if winds (a fluctuating force) happen to excite one of the resonant modes of a 
                skyscraper, this spells trouble! Having completed a brand-new skyscraper, a construction company 
                discovers a problem (oops!). When the winds pick up, the building begins to sway at dangerous 
                levels. An engineer proposes to remedy the issue by installing the following contraption at one of 
                the top floors:
            </p>
            
            <image source="images/challenge/c4skyscraper/tmd_intro.png" width="80%">
                <shortdescription>add a statement</shortdescription>
            </image>

            <p>
                This idea is called a <q>tuned mass damper</q> (TMD). It was first applied in the 1900s to reduce 
                the rolling motion of ships. Today, such systems are often included in the design of tall 
                structures. In a few cases they were indeed installed after an <q>oops</q> moment 
                (For an example, Google the story of the John Hancock Tower in Boston).

                The piston-like element in the schematic denotes viscous drag; it is a device that opposes motion 
                (of its ends relative to each other) with a force proportional to velocity:
                <m>F=-\eta v</m>. In practice, it is often indeed implemented as a piston moving in a cylinder 
                filled with oil, as drawn on the schematic.

            </p>

            <image source="images/challenge/c4skyscraper/piston.png" width="40%">
                <shortdescription>add a statement</shortdescription>
            </image>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    
<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Challenge Problem Statement</title>
        
        <p>
            This problem will use a simplified model for the tuned mass damper, as shown in the figure. The
            building, represented by the mass <m>M</m>, is driven by a force <m>F(t)</m>, and the damper is
            undriven except for its contact with the building.
        </p>
        <p>
            The coordinate <m>y</m> represents the position of the damper relative to the building. Since the
            building accelerates, this is not attached to an inertial frame, so remember that the position
            of the damper with respect to the inertial frame of the Earth is <m>x+y</m>
        </p>

        <image source="images/challenge/c4skyscraper/tmd_sketch.svg" width="80%">
                <shortdescription>add a statement</shortdescription>
        </image>
        <p>
            Here's how to proceed:
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Write the equations of motion for the building and the damper. Sanity check that
                        when <m>m_d=0</m> you just get a single equation for the building, and if the
                        building is held fixed the equation of motion for the damper makes sense.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Rewrite the equation of motion introducing the following parameters:
                        
                        <md> \dfrac{\eta}{M}\equiv\gamma,
                             \dfrac{k}{M}\equiv\omega^2,
                             \dfrac{\eta_d}{m_d} \equiv \gamma_d, 
                             \dfrac{k_d}{m_d} \equiv \omega_d^2,
                             \mu\equiv\dfrac{m_d}{M}</md>.
                        
                        Show that your equations of motion reduce to
                        <md number="no">
                            <mrow>\amp \mu\ddot{x}+\mu\ddot{y}+\gamma\dot{x}=\dfrac{F(t)}{M}</mrow>
                            <mrow>\amp \ddot{x}+\gamma_d\dot{y}=0</mrow>
                        </md>
                        
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Solve for the amplitude ratio <m>\lambda=\dfrac{A_y}{A_x}</m> of the amplitudes
                        of the building and the damper.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Use your answer to argue that we can reduce the building oscillation amplitude by using a 
                        larger mass <m>m_d</m> and/or allowing it to develop large oscillations (large <m>\lambda</m>).
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
 
    
<project>
    <p>
        This problem has a lot of algebra: be patient.
        If you use complex numbers, this will be a lot easier, but be sure you
        understand how to interpret your expressions.
    </p>

    <p>
        I cannot emphasize this enough: be strategic in approaching the algebra.
    </p>
</project>
    </paragraphs>
    </page>
</handout>
